NAME
    mv - rename or move a file

SYNOPSIS
    mv <src> <dst>

DESCRIPTION
    Renames <src> to <dst>, moving it between directories if needed. Paths
    resolve against the current directory.

    If <dst> is an existing DIRECTORY, <src> moves into it and keeps its
    basename (mv notes.txt backup/), as real mv does.

    If <dst> is an existing FILE, it is REPLACED - also as real mv does. The
    replaced file's contents are gone. Both must be ordinary files: replacing
    a directory, or moving a directory onto an existing name, is still refused
    with "already exists".

    On ext2 the replacement is very nearly atomic. The whole change is one
    write of the destination's directory entry, re-pointing it at the source's
    inode, so the name never resolves to nothing - a reader either sees the old
    file or the new one. FAT32 and exFAT cannot do this: a directory entry
    there holds the file's location itself rather than an inode number, so the
    old entry must be removed and a new one written, and between those two
    writes the name does not exist. That is a property of those formats, not of
    this implementation.

    mv x x is refused ("source and destination are the same").

SEE ALSO
    cp, rm
